      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through its chemical makeup, its unique physical properties, and its role in our world:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemistry of Water\n* **Chemical Formula:** **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$** \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **The \"Magnet\" Effect (Polarity):** Water molecules are \"polar.\" The oxygen side has a slight negative charge, while the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. Because of this, water molecules attract each other like tiny magnets, which gives water many of its unique properties (like surface tension).\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n* **Liquid:** The standard state of water found in oceans, rivers, and rain.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several unusual and miraculous properties that make life possible:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it carries vital nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through our bodies and through ecosystems.\n* **Ice Floats:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without changing its own temperature very much. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human body temperatures stable.\n\n### 4. Water on Earth\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which make up about 96.5% of all Earth's water and are salty).\n* **Freshwater:** Only about **2.5% of Earth'"
